Singapore's tourism industry is recovering strongly, with arrivals up 19 times from 2021
The Singapore Tourism Board recently announced that it expects 12-14 million international visitors to Singapore in 2023, generating around S$18-21 billion in tourism revenue; that both number of visitors and tourism revenue will return to about 67-75% of pre-COVID-19 levels in 2019; and that the tourism industry is likely to fully recover in 2024.
Singapore's tourism market is gradually restarting and recovering in 2022.
The latest statistics released by the Singapore Tourism Board show that incoming tourists will increase 19 times from 330,600 in 2021 to 6.3 million in 2022, exceeding the Board’s earlier forecast of 4 to 6 million, and that tourists have recovered to 33 percent of their pre-epidemic levels.
Singapore's tourism revenue totaled NS$8.96 billion from January to September 2022, and is expected to reach NS$13.8 billion to NS$14.3 billion for the full year 2022, a six- to seven-fold increase from NS$1.89 billion in 2021, having recovered to pre-COVID-19 levels of about 50% to 52%.
The Government of Singapore has taken a number of measures to accelerate the recovery of the tourism sector.
In April 2022, the government announced an allocation of nearly 500 million Singapore dollars to stimulate the recovery of the tourism industry.
The Tourism Board recently announced plans to allocate 110 million dollars from this budget over the next two years to launch more business and leisure activities to attract returning tourists.
To make health and wellness one of Singapore's tourism highlights, the Singapore Tourism Board has launched an intentional call for proposals on 23 November 2022 to develop a health and wellness attraction on the southern coast of Singapore.
Wang Ling Li, Executive Director of Sports and Wellness Tourism at the Singapore Tourism Board, said that in recent years, more and more tourists have put physical health at the top of their list of considerations.
The development of a health and lifestyle attraction in the South Coastal Zone, with its Bay Gardens and coastal embankment, is expected to contribute to Singapore &apos; s tourism and economy by encouraging tourists to consume and stay longer in Singapore.
The Director of the Singapore Tourism Authority, Chen Jianlong, said that health and wellness would become an important part of Singapore &apos; s tourism product in the coming years.
The COVID-19 pandemic has raised public awareness and demand for physical and mental health.
To this end, the Tourism Board has been promoting health and lifestyle programmes to the local public and international tourists over the past two years.
In June 2022, the Singapore Tourism Board hosted the first ever Healthy Health Festival, offering more than 130 health activities and experiences to the public.
Singapore will continue to offer new travel experiences to its visitors.
In addition to the newly opened Singapore Children's Museum, the Avatar World at the Bay of Plenty Gardens, the Central Beach Market in Santa Tausa, the newly added Pygmy Exploration Pygmy Exhibition Hall at the Museum of Arts and Sciences, and the newly built outdoor theatre at the Night Wildlife Park, the tourism department will support the development and renewal of projects such as the Flying Bird Park at the World of Wildlife, making Singapore an attractive international travel destination.
It is planned that Singapore will develop industry standards for meetings, exhibitions and tourism awards for sustainable development by 2023, focusing on improving waste management and carbon emissions to further optimize the tourism environment.
The recovery of Singapore's tourism industry has accelerated the recovery of the aviation industry.
Data show that as of December 18, 2022, passenger traffic at Changi Airport has recovered strongly, with up to 150,000 passengers served daily and about one million passengers per week, which is about 75% of the pre-COVID level, and the number of airline employees has returned to about 90% of pre-pandemic levels.
Data show that Singapore has received 23.6 million passengers between January and October 2022, 42% more than before the pandemic, and 170,000 flights, 53% more than before the pandemic.
The rapid recovery in tourism has also led to a recovery in accommodation, transportation, catering and shopping.
Singapore's government and tourism industry have high hopes for the Chinese market.
According to the assistant director of the International Department of the Singapore Tourism Board, Mr. Kurdi Dhan, a survey of Chinese travel platforms shows that Singapore is ranked in the top three in the world in terms of safety index, which is one of the reasons why Singapore is so popular with Chinese tourists.
Before the pandemic, China welcomed about 3 million visitors to Singapore for travel and tourism every year.
With China relaxing its border policy, Chinese tourists are expected to return this year to 30-60% of their 2019 levels.
Ko Su-kyung said that the Tourism Board will now closely monitor further signals released by China on outbound travel policies to respond to changing tourist demands.
In addition to strengthening partnerships with Chinese online travel platforms and travel agencies such as China Travel, the Singapore Tourism Board will actively prepare to welcome Chinese tourists in response to the emerging trends in traveling abroad.
Looking ahead to the 2023 tourism market, the Singapore Tourism Authority is confident that the recovery in the tourism sector will be faster than expected as long as there is no significant recession in the global economy affecting public revenues and no new large-scale outbreaks of deadly outbreaks.
